{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.049,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.09,
            0.79,
            0.175
          ],
          "content": "We know that the fraction $\\frac { 5 } { 1 0 }$ can be represented as a decimal 0.5. So, each girl will get 14 m and an additional 0.5 m of ribbon. Hence, the length of ribbon each will get is $14 + 0.5 = 14.5   m$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.186,
            0.846,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "？ Now, what if the ribbon was shared between four friends instead of 2?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.232,
            0.53,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": "So, each will get $2 9 \\div 4   \\mathrm { m }$ , that is $\\frac { 2 9 } { 4 }   \\mathrm { m }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.265,
            0.848,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "Now, the denominator of the fraction is 4. To convert a fraction to a decimal, it helps if the denominator is of the form 1, 10, 100, 1000, and so on. Can we find a fraction equivalent to $\\frac { 2 9 } { 4 }$ with such a denominator?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.342,
            0.848,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "Is 4 a factor of 10? No. Is it a factor of 100? Yes. $4 \\times 25 = 100.50$ we can get an equivalent fraction of $\\frac { 2 9 } { 4 }$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by 25."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.414,
            0.596,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{29 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\frac{725}{100} = 7.25",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.459,
            0.628,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "So each of the 4 friends will get 7.25 m of ribbon."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.503,
            0.474,
            0.525
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Division Using Place Value"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.529,
            0.847,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": "We have seen how to divide two counting numbers to get a decimal quotient. We first represented the division as a fraction. Then we found an equivalent fraction, with the denominator being of the form 1, 10, 100, 1000, and so on. It was then easy to represent this equivalent fraction as a decimal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.629,
            0.847,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "the decimal quotient."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.685,
            0.847,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": "? Suppose we want to write the quotient $\\frac { 1 0 } { 3 }$ as a decimal. Can we convert this fraction to an equivalent fraction with a denominator such as 1, 10, 100, 1000, etc.?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.76,
            0.847,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "It is not possible. So, we need a more general method to divide any two counting numbers. Let us see how we can use division using place value for this."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.817,
            0.735,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "Let us start with a quick recap of division using place value."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.934,
            0.187,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "76"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
